tel 1.0

base dendrology
import soundness

info   trees and directed acyclic graphs drawn as text: tiles computed by a diagram, characters chosen by a style
detail
    _Dendrology_ draws hierarchies in the terminal — a file listing, a dependency graph, a
    class hierarchy, a version-control history — with the box-drawing characters of `└─` and
    `├─`, and separates the two things such a drawing involves. A **diagram** computes the
    layout: for each line, the *tiles* (which connecting pieces run across it) and the node the
    line is for. A **style** maps tiles to text, so the same layout renders in box-drawing
    characters or in plain ASCII, as `Text` or as styled terminal output, by changing one
    import. A `TreeDiagram` draws a tree, and three DAG diagrams draw any graph which admits a
    topological order — an acyclicity `Dag`, a frozen `Topology` or a `Hasse` — whose nodes may
    have several parents, which a tree cannot show.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import treeStyles.squareTreeStyle

    case class Taxon(name: Text, children: List[Taxon] = Nil)

    val animalia = Taxon
      ( t"animalia",
        List(Taxon(t"chordata", List(Taxon(t"mammalia"))), Taxon(t"arthropoda")) )

    TreeDiagram.by[Taxon](_.children)(animalia).render(_.name)
    // animalia
    // ├─chordata
    // │ └─mammalia
    // └─arthropoda
    ```

    The three graph layouts answer different questions. `DagDiagram` is dense: every edge is
    drawn in a grid of connecting tiles, one line per node, which suits a small graph with
    complicated edges. `LaneDagDiagram` puts each node as a bullet on a vertical lane, with
    edges flowing between the lanes, as `git log --graph` draws history, which suits a long
    graph with few concurrent branches. `LayeredDagDiagram` arranges nodes into levels by
    depth, ordered within each level so that as few connecting lines cross as possible, which
    suits showing what depends on what rather than what happened in what order.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import strategies.throwUnsafely
    import laneDagStyles.boxDrawingLaneDagStyle
    import textMetrics.uniformMetric

    val history = Dag(t"C" -> Set(t"B"), t"B" -> Set(t"A"), t"A" -> Set())

    LaneDagDiagram(history).render(node => t" $node")
    // ●  A
    // │
    // ●  B
    // │
    // ●  C
    ```

    A style is a given, chosen by importing it by name — `import soundness.*` brings none in,
    since `import pkg.*` does not import givens — from one of three choice packages:
    `treeStyles` (`squareTreeStyle`, `roundedTreeStyle`, `asciiTreeStyle`) for a `TreeDiagram`,
    `dagStyles` (`boxDrawingDagStyle`, `asciiDagStyle`) for a `DagDiagram`, and `laneDagStyles`
    (`boxDrawingLaneDagStyle`, `asciiLaneDagStyle`) for a `LaneDagDiagram` and a
    `LayeredDagDiagram`. The lane styles measure glyphs in display cells, so they need a `Text
    is Measurable` in scope too, such as `textMetrics.uniformMetric`. Each style is
    parameterised by the line type it renders, so a diagram renders directly into styled
    terminal text with the furniture in one colour and the labels in another.

    The library is two source sets. `dendrology.tree` holds `TreeDiagram`, `TreeTile`,
    `TreeStyle`, `TextualTreeStyle`, `Expandable` and `treeStyles`; `dendrology.dag` holds the
    three DAG diagrams, `DagTile`, `DagStyle`, `LaneDagStyle`, their `Textual…` implementations
    and the `dagStyles` and `laneDagStyles` packages, and depends on `acyclicity`. Both are
    exported to the `soundness` umbrella, where the three choice packages merge with the
    umbrella's own.

refer dendrology.TreeDiagram
refer dendrology.LaneDagDiagram
refer dendrology.DagDiagram
refer dendrology.LayeredDagDiagram
refer dendrology.treeStyles
refer dendrology.laneDagStyles

entry .TreeDiagram   a tree laid out as lines of tiles, rendered through a `TreeStyle`
  detail
      A `TreeDiagram[node]` is the layout of a tree: one line per node, in depth-first order,
      each with the `TreeTile`s of its prefix — a branch or a last branch for the node itself,
      and for each ancestor an extender (if that ancestor has later siblings) or a space. It is
      built from its roots and a way of reaching children — a function, with `by`, or an
      `Expandable` instance on the node type, with `apply` — and rendered with a function from
      node to label and the `TreeStyle` in scope.

      ```scala fuel
      frame
        label The square style: right-angled corners
        code
            locally:
              import treeStyles.squareTreeStyle
              TreeDiagram.by[Taxon](_.children)(animalia).render(_.name)
              // animalia
              // ├─chordata
              // │ └─mammalia
              // └─arthropoda
      frame
        label The rounded style: the last branch curves
        code
            locally:
              import treeStyles.roundedTreeStyle
              TreeDiagram.by[Taxon](_.children)(animalia).render(_.name)
              // animalia
              // ├─chordata
              // │ ╰─mammalia
              // ╰─arthropoda
      frame
        label The ASCII style, for the plainest terminals
        code
            locally:
              import treeStyles.asciiTreeStyle
              TreeDiagram.by[Taxon](_.children)(animalia).render(_.name)
              // animalia
              // |-chordata
              // | +-mammalia
              // +-arthropoda
      ##
      ```

      The lines are a `Chain`, produced lazily: a large or deep tree is drawn as far as it is
      consumed, and an infinite tree still draws, since rendering a line needs only the
      ancestors' sibling counts, never the descendants of later nodes. A diagram is also
      `Printable` (with a `TreeStyle[Text]` in scope and a `Showable` node), so it can be
      written to `Out` directly, each node shown as `▪ node`.

      The rows are exposed — `nodes`, `tiles` and `map` — so a consumer can render lines
      itself: a coloured label for one kind of node, an annotation on another, with the
      box-drawing characters still the style's business.
  refer dendrology.TreeStyle
  refer dendrology.TreeTile
  refer dendrology.treeStyles
  refer dendrology.Expandable
  entry .apply   a diagram of the given roots, with children found through the node type's `Expandable` instance
      `TreeDiagram(roots*)` for a node type with a `given node is Expandable`: the right form
      when a node type's children are what it means by children — a filesystem path, a
      dependency, a syntax node.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      case class Taxon(name: Text, children: List[Taxon] = Nil)
      given Taxon is Expandable = _.children

      TreeDiagram(Taxon(t"a", List(Taxon(t"b")))).nodes.to[List].map(_.name)   // List(a, b)
      ```
  entry .by   a diagram of the given roots, with children found by a function
      `TreeDiagram.by[node](node => children)(roots*)`: the right form when the structure is
      already in hand, or when the same type is drawn by different relations.
  entry .printable   writes a diagram to a terminal, each node shown as `▪ node`
      Needs a `TreeStyle[Text]` in scope (one of `treeStyles`) and a `Showable` node type; the
      lines are joined with newlines.
  entry #lines   each line's tiles and node, as a lazy `Chain`
  entry #render   the lines as rendered text: each node's label, prefixed by its tiles through the `TreeStyle` in scope
      `diagram.render(node => label)`, with the label of whatever line type the style renders —
      `Text` with the `treeStyles` givens, or a styled terminal type with a style for it.
  entry #flow   the lines with each label word-wrapped to a width, continuation rows taking the style's follow-on tiles
      `diagram.flow(width)(node => label)`: each node's content flows into rows no wider than
      `width`, inclusive of its tile prefix, and the continuation rows take `TreeStyle#followOn`
      tiles — an extender continues beneath a branch, and a space follows a last child — so the
      tree's lines run on past a wrapped label. Needs a `Text is Measurable` (such as
      `textMetrics.uniformMetric`) and a `Hyphenation` in scope, and a label type which is
      `Textual` over characters.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import treeStyles.squareTreeStyle
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric

      case class Taxon(name: Text, children: List[Taxon] = Nil)

      TreeDiagram.by[Taxon](_.children)(Taxon(t"the quick brown fox"), Taxon(t"leaf"))
      . flow(11)(_.name).to[List]
      // List(├─the quick, │ brown fox, └─leaf)
      ```

      A style whose follow-on tiles are wider than its ordinary ones is measured both ways, and
      the content wraps against the tighter budget, so no row can exceed `width`.
  entry #map   each line rendered by a function of its tiles and its node
      `diagram.map(tiles => node => row)`: the general form of `render`, for a consumer which
      renders lines itself.
  entry #nodes   the node of each line, in drawing order
  entry #tiles   the tiles of each line, in drawing order

entry .TreeTile   one piece of a tree line's prefix: a branch, a last branch, an extender or a space
    An enum of four cases. `Branch` (`├─`) marks a node with later siblings and `Last` (`└─`)
    the last of its siblings; to their left, `Extender` (`│ `) carries an ancestor's line down
    past the node when that ancestor has later siblings, and `Space` fills the column when it
    does not. A `TreeStyle` says what each becomes as text.

entry .TreeStyle   the typeclass which renders a line of tree tiles and a label into one line
  detail
      `TreeStyle[line]` turns a `List[TreeTile]` and a node's label of type `line` into one
      rendered line, and is what `TreeDiagram#render` and `#flow` take from scope. It is
      parameterised by the line type, so a diagram can render into `Text` or into styled
      terminal output alike. `TextualTreeStyle` implements it for any `Textual` line type from
      four short pieces of text, which is all a new style needs; the `treeStyles` package has
      three ready-made. A style is `Findable`, so a diagram's `Printable` instance finds it in
      scope.
  refer dendrology.TextualTreeStyle
  refer dendrology.treeStyles
  entry #serialize   a line's tiles and its label as one rendered line
  entry #followOn   a continuation row of a multi-line node: the same lanes, with no branch glyph
      Used by `TreeDiagram#flow` for the second and later rows of a wrapped label: an
      extender continues, and a last child's branch gives way to space. The default repeats
      `serialize`, for a style which draws no distinct continuation prefix.

entry .TextualTreeStyle   a `TreeStyle` for any `Textual` line type, from four pieces of text
  detail
      `TextualTreeStyle(space, last, branch, extender)` gives the text of each `TreeTile`; a
      line is the tiles' texts joined, then the label. The pieces are usually two characters
      wide, so that columns align, but need not be.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given TreeStyle[Text] = TextualTreeStyle(t"  ", t"└─", t"├─", t"│ ")
      ```

      On a follow-on row, `branch` is rendered as `extender` and `last` as `space`, so the
      vertical lines continue beneath a wrapped label.
  refer dendrology.TreeStyle
  refer dendrology.treeStyles
  entry #space   the text of a `Space` tile: beneath a last child
  entry #last   the text of a `Last` tile: the branch to a node's last child
  entry #branch   the text of a `Branch` tile: the branch to a child with later siblings
  entry #extender   the text of an `Extender` tile: an ancestor's line carried down past a node
  entry #serialize   the tiles' texts joined, then the label
  entry #followOn   the tiles' follow-on texts joined, then the label
  entry #text   the text of one tile
  entry #followOnText   the text of one tile on a continuation row: `extender` for a branch, `space` for a last

entry .Expandable   the typeclass of a node type which knows its own children
    `given node is Expandable = _.children` lets `TreeDiagram(roots*)` find each node's
    children through the type rather than through a function passed at the call. `children`
    answers a `List` of the node type itself.

entry .treeStyles   the three ready-made tree styles, each a given to import by name
  detail
      `import soundness.*` brings none of these in; one is imported by name, and provides a
      `TreeStyle` for any `Textual` line type.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import treeStyles.roundedTreeStyle
      ```
  entry .squareTreeStyle   `├─`, `└─` and `│ `: right-angled corners
  entry .roundedTreeStyle   `├─`, `╰─` and `│ `: the last branch curves
  entry .asciiTreeStyle   `|-`, `+-` and `| `: ASCII only, for output which must survive any terminal

entry .DagDiagram   the dense drawing of a graph: one line per node, every edge in a grid of connecting tiles
  detail
      `DagDiagram(graph)` lays out any graph which admits a topological order — a `Dag`, a
      frozen `Topology[node]^{}` or a `Hasse` — with one line per node, in topological order
      (dependencies first), and to each line's left a column for every earlier node, holding
      the tiles of the edges which start, pass or end there. The result cannot fail: a
      `Digraph`, which may hold a cycle, has no drawing until `acyclic` has converted it. It
      renders through the `DagStyle` in scope, from the `dagStyles` package.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import dagStyles.boxDrawingDagStyle

      val types = Dag
        ( t"Any"       -> Set(),
          t"Matchable" -> Set(t"Any"),
          t"AnyVal"    -> Set(t"Matchable"),
          t"AnyRef"    -> Set(t"Matchable"),
          t"Int"       -> Set(t"AnyVal"),
          t"String"    -> Set(t"AnyRef") )

      DagDiagram(types).render(node => t"▪ $node")
      ```

      The whole layout is computed when the diagram is made, as a grid of the graph's size
      squared, which is what makes it the right form for a small graph with complicated edges
      and the wrong one for a long history; `LaneDagDiagram` is the form for that. A diagram is
      `Printable` with a `DagStyle[Text]` in scope and a `Showable` node.
  refer dendrology.DagStyle
  refer dendrology.dagStyles
  refer dendrology.LaneDagDiagram
  refer acyclicity.Dag
  entry .apply   the dense layout of any `Nodal` and `Topological` graph
      Takes the graph's `Nodal` and `Topological` instances from scope, which a `Dag`, a frozen
      `Topology` and a `Hasse` supply from their companions, so a call needs no import.
  entry .printable   writes a diagram to a terminal, each node shown as `▪ node`
      Needs a `DagStyle[Text]` (one of `dagStyles`) and a `Showable` node type.
  entry #lines   each line's tiles and node, in topological order
  entry #size   how many lines there are: one per node
  entry #render   the lines as rendered text, each node's label after its tiles, through the `DagStyle` in scope
  entry #nodes   the node of each line, in drawing order
  entry #tiles   the tiles of each line

entry .DagTile   one cell of a DAG drawing: a node, a lane, a corner, a tee, a crossing or a space
    An enum of fourteen cases, named for the compass points of their arms. `Space`;
    `Vertical` and `Horizontal` lanes; the corners `CornerNe`, `CornerNw`, `CornerSe` and
    `CornerSw`; the tees `TeeN`, `TeeS`, `TeeE` and `TeeW`; `Crossing`, a horizontal lane
    passing over a continuing vertical one without joining it; `Junction`, where lanes meet
    from all four sides; and `Node`, the bullet of a node on a lane diagram. `DagDiagram` uses
    the first eight; `LaneDagDiagram` and `LayeredDagDiagram` use all fourteen. A `DagStyle` or
    `LaneDagStyle` says what each becomes as text.

entry .DagStyle   the typeclass which renders a line of a dense `DagDiagram`
  detail
      `DagStyle[line]` turns a line's `List[DagTile]` and a node's label into one rendered line
      of type `line`, and is what `DagDiagram#render` takes from scope. `TextualDagStyle`
      implements it for any `Textual` line type from eight pieces of text; the `dagStyles`
      package has a box-drawing and an ASCII one. It is `Findable`, so the diagram's
      `Printable` instance finds it.
  refer dendrology.TextualDagStyle
  refer dendrology.dagStyles
  entry #serialize   a line's tiles and its label as one rendered line

entry .TextualDagStyle   a `DagStyle` for any `Textual` line type, from eight pieces of text
  detail
      `TextualDagStyle(space, cornerNe, vertical, teeE, horizontal, teeN, crossing, junction)`
      gives the text of each of the tiles a `DagDiagram` draws; any other `DagTile` renders as
      `space`. A line is the tiles' texts joined, then the label.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      given DagStyle[Text] =
        TextualDagStyle(t"  ", t"└─", t"│ ", t"├─", t"──", t"┴─", t"│─", t"┼─")
      ```
  refer dendrology.DagStyle
  refer dendrology.dagStyles
  entry #space   the text of a `Space` tile
  entry #cornerNe   the text of a `CornerNe` tile: an edge turning from a lane into a node's line
  entry #vertical   the text of a `Vertical` tile: a lane passing a line
  entry #teeE   the text of a `TeeE` tile: a lane with a branch to the right
  entry #horizontal   the text of a `Horizontal` tile: an edge running along a line
  entry #teeN   the text of a `TeeN` tile: an edge joining a line from above
  entry #crossing   the text of a `Crossing` tile: a lane crossed by an edge
  entry #junction   the text of a `Junction` tile: a lane joined by an edge on both sides
  entry #serialize   the tiles' texts joined, then the label
  entry #text   the text of one tile
  entry #followOnText   the text of one tile on a continuation row: a lane continues, anything else is space

entry .dagStyles   the two ready-made styles for a dense `DagDiagram`, each a given to import by name
  detail
      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import dagStyles.boxDrawingDagStyle
      ```
  entry .boxDrawingDagStyle   box-drawing characters: `└─`, `│ `, `├─`, `──`, `┴─`, `│─`, `┼─`
  entry .asciiDagStyle   ASCII only: `+-`, `| `, `--`, `|-`

entry .LaneDagDiagram   the lane drawing of a graph: nodes as bullets on vertical lanes, edges flowing between them
  detail
      `LaneDagDiagram(graph)` lays out any graph which admits a topological order — a `Dag`, a
      frozen `Topology[node]^{}` or a `Hasse` — as version-control history is drawn: each node
      is a `Node` tile on one of a few vertical lanes, in topological order from the top
      (dependencies first), and between every two node rows a connector row carries each edge
      down its lane, bending to the column of the node it reaches. An edge to the very next
      node runs straight; a longer edge takes a lane of its own, chosen nearest to its node, and
      a lane continuing past a row is drawn as `Vertical`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import laneDagStyles.boxDrawingLaneDagStyle
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric

      val diamond = Dag(t"A" -> Set(), t"B" -> Set(t"A"), t"C" -> Set(t"A"), t"D" -> Set(t"B", t"C"))

      LaneDagDiagram(diamond).render(node => t" $node")             // seven lines
      LaneDagDiagram(diamond).compact.render(node => t" $node")     // pass-through rows dropped
      ```

      A per-node glyph marks chosen nodes — the current commit, a failing build — through the
      two-function `render`, whose columns widen to fit the widest glyph, measured in display
      cells so a wide character lays out correctly:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import laneDagStyles.boxDrawingLaneDagStyle
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric

      val dag = Dag(t"A" -> Set(), t"B" -> Set(t"A"))
      val glyph = (node: Text) => if node == t"A" then t"★ " else t"● "

      LaneDagDiagram(dag).render(glyph, node => t" $node")   // List(★  A, │ , ●  B)
      ```

      The drawing cannot fail: a `Digraph` has no drawing until `acyclic` has converted it.
      It renders through the `LaneDagStyle` in scope, from the `laneDagStyles` package, which
      needs a `Text is Measurable` too; a diagram is `Printable` with a `LaneDagStyle[Text]` in
      scope and a `Showable` node, each node labelled ` node`.
  refer dendrology.LaneDagStyle
  refer dendrology.laneDagStyles
  refer dendrology.LayeredDagDiagram
  refer acyclicity.Dag
  entry .apply   the lane layout of any `Nodal` and `Topological` graph
      Takes the graph's `Nodal` and `Topological` instances from scope; a `Dag`, a frozen
      `Topology` and a `Hasse` supply them with no import. An empty graph has no lines.
  entry .printable   writes a diagram to a terminal, each node labelled ` node`
      Needs a `LaneDagStyle[Text]` (one of `laneDagStyles`, with a `Text is Measurable`) and a
      `Showable` node type.
  entry #lines   each row's tiles and, for a node row, its node; `Unset` for a connector row
  entry #size   how many rows there are, node and connector rows together
  entry #render   the rows as rendered text, with a label after each node, or with a per-node glyph too
      `render(label)` draws every node with the style's `node` glyph (`● ` in the box-drawing
      style) and appends `label(node)` to its row. `render(glyph, label)` draws each node with
      `glyph(node)` instead, widening the node's column to the widest glyph in it, measured in
      display cells by the style's `width`, so the connector rows stay aligned.
  entry #compact   the diagram with its pure pass-through connector rows dropped
      A connector row of nothing but `Vertical` lanes (more than one) and spaces says nothing
      the surrounding rows do not, so `compact` removes it; a row with exactly one lane — a
      direct edge to the next node — stays, as does any row which bends.
  entry #nodes   the node of each node row, in drawing order
  entry #tiles   the tiles of every row, connector rows included

entry .LaneDagStyle   the typeclass which renders a row of a `LaneDagDiagram` or a `LayeredDagDiagram`
  detail
      `LaneDagStyle[line]` turns a row's `List[DagTile]`, the glyphs to draw at particular
      columns, each column's width and an optional label into one rendered line of type
      `line`, and measures a glyph's display width so that columns can be sized to fit.
      `TextualLaneDagStyle` implements it for any `Textual` line type from fourteen pieces of
      text; the `laneDagStyles` package has a box-drawing and an ASCII one. It is `Findable`,
      so the diagrams' `Printable` instances find it.
  refer dendrology.TextualLaneDagStyle
  refer dendrology.laneDagStyles
  entry #serialize   a row's tiles, its glyphs by column, the column widths and an optional label as one line
  entry #width   the display width of a glyph, for sizing its column

entry .TextualLaneDagStyle   a `LaneDagStyle` for any `Textual` line type, from fourteen pieces of text
  detail
      `TextualLaneDagStyle(space, vertical, horizontal, cornerNe, cornerNw, cornerSe, cornerSw,
      teeN, teeS, teeE, teeW, junction, crossing, node)` gives the text of each `DagTile`, and
      takes a `Text is Measurable` to measure glyphs in display cells rather than code units,
      so wide characters and combining marks lay out correctly. Each piece is two characters:
      the first is the cell, and the second is repeated to pad the column to its width, so a
      `──` horizontal stretches and a `│ ` lane does not.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric

      given LaneDagStyle[Text] = TextualLaneDagStyle
        ( t"  ", t"│ ", t"──", t"╰─", t"╯ ", t"╭─", t"╮ ",
          t"┴─", t"┬─", t"├─", t"┤ ", t"┼─", t"──", t"● " )
      ```
  refer dendrology.LaneDagStyle
  refer dendrology.laneDagStyles
  entry #space   the text of a `Space` tile
  entry #vertical   the text of a `Vertical` tile: a lane passing a row
  entry #horizontal   the text of a `Horizontal` tile: an edge running along a row
  entry #cornerNe   the text of a `CornerNe` tile: an edge from above turning right
  entry #cornerNw   the text of a `CornerNw` tile: an edge from above turning left
  entry #cornerSe   the text of a `CornerSe` tile: an edge from the right turning down
  entry #cornerSw   the text of a `CornerSw` tile: an edge from the left turning down
  entry #teeN   the text of a `TeeN` tile: a horizontal run joined from above
  entry #teeS   the text of a `TeeS` tile: a horizontal run branching down
  entry #teeE   the text of a `TeeE` tile: a lane branching right
  entry #teeW   the text of a `TeeW` tile: a lane joined from the left
  entry #junction   the text of a `Junction` tile: lanes meeting from all four sides
  entry #crossing   the text of a `Crossing` tile: a horizontal run passing over a continuing lane
  entry #node   the text of a `Node` tile: the default bullet, used when no glyph is given for the node
  entry #width   the display width of a glyph, through the `Text is Measurable` the style was made with
  entry #serialize   each tile's cell padded to its column's width, a glyph in place of a node's bullet, then the label
  entry #text   the text of one tile

entry .laneDagStyles   the two ready-made styles for lane and layered diagrams, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Each needs a `Text is Measurable` in scope to resolve, such as `textMetrics.uniformMetric`
      (one cell per character) or `textMetrics.wideCharacterWidthMetric` (two for an East Asian
      wide character).

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import laneDagStyles.boxDrawingLaneDagStyle
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric
      ```
  entry .boxDrawingLaneDagStyle   box-drawing characters with rounded corners, and `● ` for a node
  entry .asciiLaneDagStyle   ASCII only: `| `, `--`, `+ ` for every corner and tee, and `* ` for a node

entry .LayeredDagDiagram   the layered drawing of a graph: nodes in levels by depth, ordered so that few edges cross
  detail
      `LayeredDagDiagram(graph)` lays out any graph which admits a topological order — a `Dag`,
      a frozen `Topology[node]^{}` or a `Hasse` — by the graph's `layered` arrangement: each
      node in a level, dependencies above dependents, the nodes within a level ordered to
      minimise crossings, and an edge spanning several levels passing straight through the
      levels between. Each level is a row of `Node` tiles at their columns, and between levels
      a connector row bends each edge from its upper column to its lower one. A node takes the
      median column of its neighbours in the level above, so a straight edge stays straight.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import laneDagStyles.boxDrawingLaneDagStyle
      import textMetrics.uniformMetric

      val diamond = Dag(t"A" -> Set(), t"B" -> Set(t"A"), t"C" -> Set(t"A"), t"D" -> Set(t"B", t"C"))

      LayeredDagDiagram(diamond).render(node => t"● $node  ")   // A; then B and C; then D
      ```

      The `Ranking` in scope decides the levels: by default a node sits one level below its
      deepest dependency, so every node depending on nothing shares the first row;
      `import rankings.balancedRanking` pulls each node with dependants down to the level
      above its shallowest dependant, which shortens a long edge from a source that only feeds
      something deep. The rendered rows of a given graph therefore depend on the ranking and on
      the crossing minimisation, so a stored expectation must be regenerated when either
      changes. It renders through the `LaneDagStyle` in scope, as `LaneDagDiagram` does, and is
      `Printable` with a `LaneDagStyle[Text]` and a `Showable` node, each node shown as `● node`.
  refer dendrology.LaneDagStyle
  refer dendrology.laneDagStyles
  refer acyclicity.Layering
  refer acyclicity.Ranking
  entry .apply   the layered layout of any `Nodal` and `Topological` graph, by the `Ranking` in scope
      Takes the graph's `Nodal` and `Topological` instances and a `Ranking`
      (`Ranking.LongestPath` from the companion unless `rankings.balancedRanking` is imported).
      An empty graph has no rows.
  entry .printable   writes a diagram to a terminal, each node shown as `● node`
      Needs a `LaneDagStyle[Text]` (one of `laneDagStyles`, with a `Text is Measurable`) and a
      `Showable` node type.
  entry #rows   each row's tiles and the nodes at its columns; a connector row has none
  entry #size   how many rows there are, level and connector rows together
  entry #render   the rows as rendered text, each node drawn by a glyph function, columns widened to fit
      `render(glyph)`: every node is drawn as `glyph(node)` at its column, and each column is
      as wide as the widest glyph in it, measured in display cells by the style's `width`, so
      the connector rows stay aligned. There is no separate label: a glyph such as `t"● $node  "`
      carries it.
  entry #tiles   the tiles of every row, connector rows included
  entry #nodesAt   for each row, the nodes by column; empty for a connector row
